Here is a simple script that creates a form using HTML and JavaScript that accepts user input and saves it to a text file:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Text Input Form</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Text Input Form</h1>
  <form id="input-form">
    <label for="input-text">Enter some text:</label>
    <input type="text" id="input-text" name="input-text">
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
  </form>
  <br>
  <h2>Saved Text:</h2>
  <textarea id="saved-text" name="saved-text" readonly></textarea>
  <script>
    const form = document.getElementById("input-form");
    const inputText = document.getElementById("input-text");
    const savedText = document.getElementById("saved-text");

    form.addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
      event.preventDefault();

      const inputTextValue = inputText.value;
      savedText.value = inputTextValue;

      const textFile = new File([inputTextValue], "input.txt", { type: "text/plain" });
      const input = document.createElement("input");
      input.type = "file";
      input.value = textFile;

      form.appendChild(input);
      form.submit();
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This script creates a form with a text input field and a submit button. When the user enters some text and clicks the submit button, the script saves the inputted text to a text file and displays it in a textarea element.

Note that this script uses the File API to create a file input element and submit the form. This API is not supported in all browsers, so you may need to use a polyfill or fallback to a different approach if you need to support older browsers.
